[Music]  [Music]  [Music]  before during and after World War two  there was a widespread perception the  aeronautics faced a so-called sound  barrier at speeds around 767 miles per  hour  also known as Mach 1 there were at least  three obstacles first it was common  knowledge that lift started to decrease  at very high subsonic Mach numbers above  about Mach point-eight second at the  same time the drag started to increase  substantially and third not only was it  harder to stay aloft and move forward  there was also a significant loss of  control that pilots encountered during  high speed test dives for example on  November 4th 1941 shortly before Pearl  Harbor  Ralph Reardon had conducted a high-speed  dive in a Lockheed p-38 developed almost  four years by one of the most famous  aircraft designers of all time Clarence  Kelly Johnson the plane shook the  controls locked up and the tail suddenly  ripped off resulting in a fiery crash  into a house in Glendale California and  what made all this even more puzzling  was that at flight speeds beyond the  speed of sound  scientists knew from ballistic testing  that the aerodynamic drag coefficient  actually came back down as the Mach  number continued to increase deeper into  the supersonic regime it was as if there  was some kind of inherent physical limit  a little sound barrier the prevented  flight near or add the speed of sound  it seemed that supersonic flight was  possible but how to transition through  what came to be called the transonic  regime was a huge concern if bullets  could go faster than sound why not  airplanes the breakthrough came when the  National Advisory Committee for  Aeronautics or NACA which was the  predecessor to NASA built special  transonic wind tunnels to study the  phenomena happening at transonic speeds  and what they found was startling when  the airspeed increased above a certain  threshold shockwaves began to form on  the wing and as the velocity continued  to increase the strength of the shock  increased and its location on the wing  changed they found that the location of  the shock was very sensitive to  parameters such as the flow velocity and  the wing angle of attack the implication  of this was huge for flight approaching  the speed of sound the presence of the  shock on the body would dramatically  change the distribution of pressure on  the wing as the shock position moved  further aft on the wing which would  happen at higher flight speeds a region  of low pressure also moved further and  further aft on the wing  this aft loading of the airfoil changes  the center of pressure on the upper  surface this change in location of the  net force would in turn change the  moment acting on the wing making the  wing more nose-heavy at the same time  the presence of the shock would  dramatically increase the drag when the  air flows across a shock it experiences  a very rapid increase in pressure this  is an adverse pressure gradient which  tends to cause the flow to separate so  with a shock present on the wings  boundary layer separation is much more  likely to occur then when the boundary  layer separates the wake thickens  dramatically and pressure drag goes way  up this was one of the reasons why they  saw a dramatic increase in drag the  closer they got to the speed of sound  the negative consequences of shocks on  the aircraft were later defined by the  famous Theodore von Karman in his  messanger lecture at Cornell University  in 1953 first the appearance of a shock  on the wing can lead to a sudden change  in the trim of the aircraft which forces  it out of balance second the presence of  shocks on the control surfaces can make  a dramatic increase in the force  required on the stick to keep the  aircraft under control and the control  surfaces can experience an uncommanded  deflection sending the aircraft out of  control and third strong vibrations  could be encountered as the shock  bounces around on the aircraft surface  all three of these critical features  could be present at once imagine that  you're the test pilot of an aircraft and  advancing the throttle with trepidation  to higher and higher Mach number  suddenly you encounter significant  buffeting of the aircraft and lock of  the control surfaces panic would set in  since the scientific community at that  time did not yet understand the physical  causes of these events compressibility  effects were shrouded in great mystery  so it's no wonder that the community at  large considered the speed of sound to  be a barrier to high-speed flight the  loss of lift the significant increase in  drag an extremely hazardous impacts on  aircraft stability and control were  indeed dangerous and appeared  unconquerable now at a simple level the  speed of sound and how it's different  from the speed of light is familiar from  thunderstorms if you see a flash of  lightning and then count once per second  until you hear the clap of thunder  corresponding to the flash you can tell  how far away that particular lightning  strike was the general rule of thumb is  one mile of separation will give a  five-second delay between the lightning  strike and when we hear it this rule of  thumb states that sound travels 5,280  feet or one mile within five seconds  this gives the speed of sound of 1,050  six feet per second which is exactly  what the speed of sound is on a very  cold day with a temperature of five  degrees Fahrenheit on a 70 degree day  the speed of sound is a little faster at  11:28 feet per second or 770 miles per  hour so the old rule of thumb is fairly  accurate now let's think about how sound  propagates through air a pressure  disturbance at a particular location is  communicated to the surrounding air by  intermolecular collisions more frequent  collisions lead to faster propagation of  the sound wave the most dominant factor  for the rate of collisions is the  temperature of the gas which is a  measure of the internal energy of the  gas the average molecular velocity which  dictates the frequency of collisions in  a gas depends on the square root of  temperature so the speed of sound also  depends on the square root of absolute  temperature so the atmosphere gets  hotter the speed of sound also increases  slightly the Mach number is simply the  ratio of velocity to the local speed of  sound a Mach number of one a flight  speed equal to the speed of sound while  any Mach number less than that is  subsonic and any value above one is  supersonic besides being an indicator of  the sonic condition Mach number also  tells us the relative importance of  compressibility effects at a Mach number  below about 0.3 any compressibility  effects are negligible and engineers  often assume that air density is  constant between Mach point 3 and about  point eight compressibility effects  become increasingly dominant and must be  accounted for above about Mach point  eight local shockwaves are usually  present and the drag on a flight vehicle  increases dramatically from Mach point  eight to about one point two this regime  is referred to as the transonic regime a  phrase jointly coined by Theodore von  Karman and Hugh Dryden compressibility  effects and why shocks form can be  visualized more easily by simplifying  the situation to an acoustic point  source that can move through the air the  point source radiates pulses of acoustic  energy at equally spaced time intervals  if the acoustic source is not moving  then all of the disturbance waves  propagate out radially and uniformly  each radial location receives the sound  wave at the same time but if the sound  source is moving say it half the speed  of sound then the sound waves no longer  radiate equally in all directions the  sound waves on the upstream side of the  motion are bunched up with the spacing  that is half of what it was in the  quiescent case you can think of it as  the sound source chasing the sound wave  that it just emitted but is traveling  only half as fast as the wave  propagation speed this is the same  physical reason for the Doppler effect  where the frequency of an oncoming  source of sound is shifted higher if the  sound source is moving with a speed  equal to the speed of sound it is no  longer possible for sound energy to  propagate upstream  since the sound source is moving at  exactly the same speed as the wave  propagation speed the snow upstream  position is aware of the sound source  until the sound source is actually there  an upstream observe observer would not  be able to detect the sound source until  it arrived we call this a zone of  silence  lastly suppose the sound source is  moving at a speed that is twice the  speed of sound clearly an adjacent  observer cannot detect the sound source  until well after its arrival the zone of  silence now extends to include a wider  region around the source where only  positions within a small downstream cone  can detect the presence of the source  for speed of Mach 2 the half angle of  this cone is only 30 degrees as Theodore  von Karman is equipped nobody ever heard  the bullet that killed him  since the bullet travels at a speed well  above the speed of sound it would be  impossible for someone to hear the  gunshot before being struck by the  bullet for an aircraft in flight it's  helpful to think of the aircraft is  pushing its way through the air with the  air molecules forced out of the way to  accommodate the presence of the aircraft  but it speeds closer to the speed of  sound movement of the aircraft through  the air produces disturbances pressure  waves which propagate ahead of the  aircraft and communicate to the upstream  air molecules about the presence of the  oncoming vehicle this upstream  propagation of information always occurs  at the speed of sound as the flight  vehicle moves faster with a speed that  approaches the speed of sound there is  less time available for the upstream air  molecules to respond to the pressure  disturbances thus the air molecules tend  to compress but they bunch up more than  they would in an incompressible flow so  the density of air increases immediately  in front of the aircraft this  compression of air results in greater  deflection of the streamlines and a  thickening of the effective wing shape  the effects of cabeza bility can be  conveyed by a simple relationship that  was independently derived in germany and  england referred to as the prandtl  glauert compressibility correction this  rule predicts that there will be an  increase in lift as  free stream Mach number increases since  the quantity in the denominator  decreases as Mach number goes up this  rule is really an approximation but it  allows us to appreciate the impact of  increasing flight Mach number as the  free stream Mach number increases the  upstream propagation of information is  inhibited since the aircraft speed is  approaching the speed of sound the  distance between successive pressure  waves decreases and the disturbances  start to coalesce and finally when the  Flavian reaches the speed of sound it is  no longer possible for the disturbance  information to propagate upstream these  disturbances coalesce into a shock wave  which has a sudden change in physical  properties there is a nearly  instantaneous jump in velocity pressure  temperature and density across a shock  wave it's interesting to note that shock  waves can form on a vehicle well before  the flight speed of the vehicle goes  supersonic this is because the local  flow velocity around portions of the  wing can be much higher than the free  stream velocity since the flow  accelerates around the wing in fact it's  very common for commercial airliners  which cruise at about Mach 0.82 to have  shocks forming on the wings this is a  common feature the transonic regime the  presence of local air flows greater than  the speed of sound for an aircraft whose  overall movement is less than the speed  of sound in fact these shock waves can  be visible for the passengers sitting  near the wing if the angle of the Sun is  just right the density gradient across  the shock will refract light by  differing angles and a shadow will be  cast on the wing surface due to the  presence of the shock when an aircraft's  flight speed exceeds the speed of sound  then a shock wave will form at the nose  of the aircraft and usually at other  locations on the aircraft body this  shock wave can be visualized by the  refraction of light through the density  gradient resulting from the shock the  reason for the refraction of light  across a shock is the exact same reason  why we see a mirage over a roadway or a  desert on a hot day the hot air rising  from these surfaces is much less dense  than the surrounding air and the light  is refracted by a different amount  this effect called the schlieren  technique has enabled some interesting  methods for visualizing the flow in fact  the schlieren technique was one of the  key tools that maca researchers used in  the 1940s to develop an understanding of  shocks and compressibility effects much  more recently nasa's developed a  technique where a chase plane with a  high-speed camera images light rays from  a cluttered background that have passed  through the flow field around an  aircraft post-processing of these images  to extract density gradients results in  striking visualizations of the shock  wave pattern around modern supersonic  aircraft we can see shockwaves forming  on the aircraft nose at the engine  inlets at the wing body junctions at the  tail and at the engine nozzle and just a  short distance away from the aircraft we  can also observe two separate shockwaves  merging into a single one farther away  from the aircraft this process continues  until there are two predominant waves  one form from the nose and the other  from the tail the combination of these  two disturbances produces what is  referred to as an N wave so named due to  the characteristic shape of its pressure  time history this N wave has two very  strong pressure jumps that happen in a  short amount of time as the aircraft  flies above some object on the ground  this is exactly what a sonic boom is  sometimes observers on the ground can  even separately discern the two pressure  jumps within a single n wave leading to  the characteristic double boom from the  Space Shuttle on re-entry after  supersonic flight became possible in  1940s it quickly became apparent that  this sonic boom could have very  destructive consequences one early  example was a ceremonial flyover of a  four-ship of f-105 thunderchief fighter  aircraft over the US Air Force Academy  on May 31st 1968 pilots of these  commemoration flights liked to push the  boundaries perhaps in an effort to  one-up their colleagues so there's a  push to greater flight speeds on these  low-level flyovers well for this  demonstration in 1968 the pilot came in  a bit too low and a bit  to fast as the f-105 zoomed in it  cleared the buildings on campus by only  about 50 feet and then pulled up with  full afterburner on this manoeuvre  caused the aircraft to momentarily  exceed the speed of sound causing a  relatively strong sonic boom at low  altitude and the result was catastrophic  over 300 windows at the academy were  broken and at least 15 people were  injured by flying shards of broken glass  the total repair bill was estimated at  over a quarter million dollars for this  high-speed stunt so in order to prevent  such incidents as well as because of  concerns about noise the FAA instituted  a ban in 1973 on supersonic flight of  non-military aircraft over the  continental United States when the  Concorde was flying the pilots had to  decelerate to subsonic speeds before  they could fly over the u.s. limiting  the high-speed flight only to regions  over the ocean NASA and industry have  worked on concepts to reduce the  strength of the sonic boom through  shaping of the N wave the quiet  supersonic technology explained  demonstrator is shaped such that the  magnitude of the overpressure generated  by the N wave is reduced and spread out  over time such that the shaped signature  will be less like a boom and more like a  soft thump let's now turn our attention  back to the world war ii era to discuss  how aircraft design was radically  changed in order to handle the effects  of compressibility and shockwaves with  propeller driven aircraft going faster  and faster the first sign of  compressibility effects and locally  supersonic flow occurred on the  propeller blades here the rotational  speed of the blade is combined with the  forward flight speed of the aircraft to  make for a very high Mach number taking  relative velocity at the blade tips well  above the speed of sound  this results in significant drag  ultimately requiring much more torque to  drive the propeller so propellers were  becoming a limiting factor and the quest  to go fast  fortuitously the limitations of  propellers coincided with the invention  of the jet engine  suddenly aircraft designers could attain  dramatic increases in maximum flight  speeds where the maximum speed jumps  from 469 miles per hour with the  propeller driven Messerschmitt 209 in  1939 to 606 miles per hour with the jet  driven Gloster Meteor F mark 4 in 1945  for the next 31 years the increases just  kept coming but the engine that set the  stage for the first supersonic flight  known as breaking the sound barrier of  roughly 770 miles per hour was a rocket  engine on October 14 1947 over Rogers  dry lake bed in the Mojave Desert Chuck  Yeager strapped into the bell x-1 for  that record attempt the x1 simply put  was a sleek rocket with wings that were  solely designed for speed indeed it was  powered by a rocket engine with a fixed  supply of fuel onboard and the  efficiency associated with rocket  propulsion flight for long periods could  not be sustained but it was long enough  a b-29 Superfortress created the x1  aloft to an altitude of 20,000 feet  where it was released from underneath  the wing after a momentary delay Yeager  fired the rocket motor and off he went  anticipating the potential for loss of  control Yeager successively accelerated  to a Mach number of 0.8 3.88 and point 9  to gingerly testing the elevator at each  condition looking for any sign of  control lockup or loss of control  effectiveness that were so often  encountered in that era finding no  anomalies  he continued to push the Mach number  even higher while Adam altitude of  42,000 feet the Mach number pushed up to  a value of 0.98 fluctuated momentarily  and then jumped to a value off the scale  post flight data analysis indicated that  he had accelerated as fast as Mach 1.06  he had done it in this moment Yeager  became the first human being to pilot  the flight vehicle faster than the speed  of sound and he lived to tell about it  suddenly the idea of asan bearer was not  so threatening years flight cleared the  way for a continued push towards  over faster flight speeds in the quest  for scientific understanding and higher  performance flight the flight Mach  number where shocks initially appear at  some point on the aircraft is called the  critical condition aerodynamicists  invest a lot of effort in pushing the  critical Mach number as high as possible  to enable the aircraft to fly even  faster and delaying the onset of shocks  and the associated drag rise there are a  number of techniques and tricks there  dynamicists can use to push the critical  Mach number higher delaying the problems  induced by compressibility effects  before and during World War two the  Germans came across an interesting idea  for reducing the wave drag on an  high-speed aircraft using swept-back  wings this idea was first presented by  Adolf Boozman at the 1935  invitation-only Volta conference he  proposed an idea stating that  compressibility effects on a wing were  due to the Mach number of the flow  perpendicular to the leading edge so if  the wing were swept back the component  of flow velocity parallel to the leading  edge would not have an impact leaving  only the component perpendicular to the  leading edge to affect the wave drag  this allowed a designer to sweep the  wings back and increase the flight Mach  number at which shocks and other  compressibility effects would be  encountered interestingly the American  researchers who attended the conference  didn't really pick up on the idea and  promptly forgot about it however within  Germany the idea resonated and they ran  with it this led to a substantial  investment of energy and research  dollars to investigate the effects of  swept wings on high-speed aerodynamics  it wasn't until the closing days of  World War two that the Americans caught  on to the idea in an effort to capture  as much German aerodynamic know-how as  possible at the conclusion of the war  the u.s. sent a delegation of top  scientists and engineers to Germany led  by Theodore von Karman one of their  visits was to a German research shop at  Braunschweig on May 7th  the day before VE Day at Braun schwag  they stumbled upon reams of data and  wind tunnel models that showed strong  evidence of a sustained research program  into  high-speed flight the effects of wing  sweep were immediately apparent to the  American visitors one member of the team  was George Shire an engineer from Boeing  almost immediately after seeing the  German data for the first time he wrote  a detailed letter back to his home  office describing what he had seen  he breathlessly explained the concept of  the swept wing in his letter knowing  that the b-47 bomber was an ongoing  design project within the company that  would immediately benefit from this idea  Boeing engineers them throughout their  conceptual design of a straight wing  bomber and started over with a new swept  wing design the b-47 then went on to  become a highly successful jet bomber  that served at the outset of the Cold  War  one key aircraft that directly flowed  from the b-47 was the ubiquitous b-52  bomber which first flew in 1952 and was  still flying well into the 21st century  in fact the b-52 design is so enduring  that it is projected to continue flying  at least until 2040 for another key  aircraft that directly descended from  the b-47 is the 707 the Boeing company's  very first commercial jet airliner so  the entire lineage of Boeing's  commercial airliners with swept wings  can trace their roots to the b-47 and  ultimately to a doff Guzman's  aerodynamic work for increasing the  critical Mach number the thickness of  the wing is another key aspect of the  wing design that has a huge impact on  shock development and the critical Mach  number with all other factors being  equal a thicker wing will induce more  curvature of the streamlines which in  turn accentuates the compressibility  effects a thick wing will have a lower  critical Mach number meaning that shocks  will initially appear at lower flight  speeds so many of the earliest  supersonic aircraft had extremely thin  wings for example the wing on the f-104  starfighter was so thin that the leading  edge thickness was a razor-sharp  fraction of a millimeter thick and had  to be protected during ground handling  both for the aircraft and for the ground  crew the disadvantage of a thin wing  however is that there  is much less room available for storing  fuel lighting systems instrumentation  and actuators for the control services  so an alternative to extremely thin  wings was developed the supercritical  airfoil this idea can also trace its  lineage back to the German  aerodynamicists in the 1940s but is most  popularly associated with the NASA  researcher named Richard Wickham the  supercritical airfoil is an interesting  design where the leading edge is in the  elliptical shape and the upper surface  has a long and flat roof top this shape  produces rapid acceleration but then a  long sustained region of relatively  constant pressure the idea is to keep  the peak pressure below the critical  pressure such that the local velocity  remains subsonic over most the airfoil  surface by delaying shock development to  higher free stream Mach numbers the  supercritical airfoil incurs less drag  at a particular flight condition  supercritical airfoils are now widely  used on most commercial airliners since  they offer such strong drag benefits it  turns out that Whitcomb also played a  pivotal role in the development of a key  aircraft prototype in the late 1940s the  US Air Force determined that a new  interceptor aircraft was required with  the capability to fly at supersonic  speeds convair won the competition for  the Interceptor and developed the YF 102  for this purpose however when they  started test flights of the aircraft the  transonic drag rise was much higher than  anticipated  in fact the aircraft could not even  exceed the speed of sound with a maximum  Mach number 0.98 this is where Wickham's  ideas came into play he had just put  forward his concept for an area rule  which basically states that transonic  drag could be minimized by avoiding  discontinuities in the cross sectional  area of an aircraft the idea is to keep  the cross-sectional area as constant as  possible along the length of the  aircraft the presence of the wings that  mid fuselage will obviously increase the  cross-sectional area the only way to  minimize the cross-sectional area  increase was to reduce the size of the  fuselage in the vicinity of the wing  this led to the design  of area ruled fuselages which took on a  waist like shape not unlike a  traditional coke bottle the outcome of  this development for the YF 102 was a  dramatic improvement in performance the  y f-102a which employed the area ruled  fuselage had a larger engine could now  achieve a maximum Mach number of 1.2 to  this development saved the design from  the chopping block and convair went on  to produce 1000 f-102 Delta Dagger x'  thanks to Wickham's innovative idea so  with all of these advancements in  aerodynamics what is the maximum speed  of an aircraft today the well-known  sr-71 blackbird is the current record  holder with a maximum recorded speed of  2,000 193 miles per hour set in 1976 a  little over Mach 2.85 unofficially  however it is widely known that the  aircraft can fly much faster one  blackbird pilot reported going as fast  as Mach 3.5 in order to evade a missile  over Libya the sr-71 illustrates very  well how shockwaves drive the design of  high speed flight vehicles in some  interesting ways oblique shocks or  shockwaves oriented at an angle with  respect to the flow direction are pinned  to sharp edges oblique shocks have much  less drag than bow shocks which form a  head of blunt surfaces but they do  induce increased aerodynamic heating so  drag reduction at the expense of  increased aerodynamic heating is the  main reason why supersonic aircraft have  pointy noses and sharp spikes in front  of the engines an sr-71 there's a long  conical four body on each engine which  is specifically designed for forming an  oblique shock at the front if the  conical four body were not present then  a bow shock would form which would  produce much higher drag the angle of  the oblique shock depends on the angle  of the fore body and on the oncoming  Mach number of the flow higher Mach  numbers result in smaller shock wave  angles the optimum angle for the shock  wave should be designed into the flight  vehicle such that the drag is minimized  and Inlet conditions for the engine are  optimized but with an aircraft such as  the sr-71 there are interesting  challenges that arise when the aircraft  traverses a large range of flight Mach  numbers the pointed four body on each  engine on the sr-71 actually moves in  flight adjusting with the flight Mach  number in order to maintain optimal flow  conditions for the engine despite the  fact that the sr-71 was designed in the  early 1960s it's 1976 speed record when  unsurpassed for over 40 years it was and  is an incredibly advanced aircraft  design and bringing things full circle  the designer of the sr-71 was none other  than Kelly Johnson the very same  designer of the p-38 lightning then  encountered such severe compressibility  effects back in 1941 this is a testament  to the incredible pace of innovation and  also to the amazing talent of Johnson  and his team at the Lockheed skunkworks  it is truly remarkable how much that  Kelly Johnson and the entire  aeronautical community learned about  compressibility effects in high-speed  flight in the few intervening years  between the start of World War two and  the sr-71 design of the 1960s so what  factors limit the maximum speed of sr-71  design there are two significant  limiting factors materials and  propulsion systems advanced materials  are required that can withstand the high  temperatures associated with  aerothermodynamic heating at such high  flight speeds the stagnation temperature  is incredibly high forcing the sr-71 to  rely on titanium and composite materials  for maintaining a lightweight design and  effective strength at high temperature  but even these advanced materials have  limits on strength and heating  traditional jet engine propulsion  systems become less effective at high  Mach numbers but while Chuck Yeager used  the Rockets overcome this problem  high-speed air-breathing propulsion  systems are also possible such as Ram  Jets and scram Jets the main idea of a  ramjet is to achieve compression of the  air by relying on the high speed of the  Flavian to  provide the driving force for  compression a scramjet is based on the  same idea except the combustion process  happens at supersonic speeds within the  engine ramjet and scramjet concepts only  work well as supersonic or hypersonic  speeds but are the most efficient  concepts at high speed so the next world  record for a piloted aircraft will  likely rely on a scramjet engine

